            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Breaks a matrix into Q, whose columns are directions all at right angles to one another and each of length 1, and R, an upper triangular matrix, so that the matrix equals Q times R.\n\nQ's columns span the same directions as the matrix's columns, but squared up: the first is the first column made length 1, the second is the second column with any part along the first removed, and so on. That is how a rough frame of axes - three vectors from a sensor or a body segment that are nearly but not quite at right angles - is turned into a proper rotation. R records how the original columns were built from the clean ones.\n\nIt is also a numerically safe way to solve least-squares problems.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.linalg.qr\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nmatrix:\nAny 2-D matrix (or a stack of them). Every input is decomposed and sent.\n\nmode:\nAn option, under the node. reduced (the default) gives Q with as many columns as the matrix; complete gives a square Q.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nQ:\nThe squared-up directions, one per column.\n\nR:\nThe upper triangular matrix. Sent first, then Q.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe 3 x 2 matrix 1 2 / 3 4 / 5 6. Q's two columns are at right angles and length 1;\nR is upper triangular (its lower left is 0). np.matmul of Q and R gives back the original matrix. A metro re-sends it five times a second.\n\nSIGNS:\nNumPy may return a column of Q negated, with the matching row of R negated too; the product is the same.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.linalg.svd and np.linalg.eigh find a matrix's principal directions rather than squaring up its own. np.linalg.lstsq solves least squares directly. t.linalg.qr is the PyTorch version.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1000,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Breaks a matrix into Q, whose columns are directions all at right angles to one another and each of length 1, and R, an upper triangular matrix, so that the matrix equals Q times R.\n\nQ's columns span the same directions as the matrix's columns, but squared up: the first is the first column made length 1, the second is the second column with any part along the first removed, and so on. That is how a rough frame of axes - three vectors from a sensor or a body segment that are nearly but not quite at right angles - is turned into a proper rotation. R records how the original columns were built from the clean ones.\n\nIt is also a numerically safe way to solve least-squares problems.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.linalg.qr\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nmatrix:\nAny 2-D matrix (or a stack of them). Every input is decomposed and sent.\n\nmode:\nAn option, under the node. reduced (the default) gives Q with as many columns as the matrix; complete gives a square Q.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nQ:\nThe squared-up directions, one per column.\n\nR:\nThe upper triangular matrix. Sent first, then Q.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe 3 x 2 matrix 1 2 / 3 4 / 5 6. Q's two columns are at right angles and length 1;\nR is upper triangular (its lower left is 0). np.matmul of Q and R gives back the original matrix. A metro re-sends it five times a second.\n\nSIGNS:\nNumPy may return a column of Q negated, with the matching row of R negated too; the product is the same.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.linalg.svd and np.linalg.eigh find a matrix's principal directions rather than squaring up its own. np.linalg.lstsq solves least squares directly. t.linalg.qr is the PyTorch version."
        },
